kwargs.content:
  Act as an ontology engineer. Use the following content (examples, part of the ontology, and a list of guidelines) as a guide to translate the natural language rule you are given into a SWRL rule. 
  EXAMPLES
    Example 1
    Rule in natural language: If a person has a sibling who is a man, then the person has a brother.
    SWRL rule: Person(?p) ^ hasSibling(?p, ?s) ^ Person(?s) ^ IsMan(?s, true) -> hasBrother(?p, ?s)

    Example 2
    Rule in natural language: if a person has a phone number that starts with '+', then the person has an international phone number.
    SWRL rule: Person(?p) ^ hasPhoneNumber(?p, ?number) ^ swrlb:startsWith(?number, "+") -> hasInternationalPhoneNumber(?p, true)

    Example 3
    Rule in natural language:  if a person has age greater than 17, then the person is an adult and has id card.
    SWRL rule: Person(?p) ^ hasAge(?p, ?age) ^ Age(?age) ^ swrlb:greaterThan(?age, 17) ^ IdCard(?id) -> Adult(?p) ^ hasIdCard(?p, ?id)

    Example 4
    Rule in natural language: if a publication has two different authors, then those authors have cooperated with each other.
    SWRL rule: Publication(?p) ^ Author(?y) ^ Author(?z) ^ hasAuthor(?p, ?y) ^ hasAuthor(?p, ?z) ^ differentFrom(?y, ?z) -> cooperatedWith(?y, ?z)

    Example 5
    Rule in natural language: if a researcher has published more than 10 papers in a Top-Tier conference, then the researcher is a prominent researcher and has a high citation impact.
    SWRL rule: Researcher(?r) ^ hasPublished(?r, ?c) ^ Conference(?c) ^ topTierConference(?c) ^ swrlb:greaterThan(count(?c), 10) -> ProminentResearcher(?r) ^ hasHighCitationImpact(?r, true)
  END OF EXAMPLES
  PART OF ONTOLOGY 
    ###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#hasIncidentImpact
beAWARE_ontology:hasIncidentImpact rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                   owl:inverseOf beAWARE_ontology:isImpactOfIncident ;
                                   rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:Incident ;
                                   rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:Impact ;
                                   rdfs:label "has incident impact" ;
                                   skos:definition "Indicates the impact of the specific incident." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#hasImpactOn
beAWARE_ontology:hasImpactOn rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                             owl:inverseOf beAWARE_ontology:isImpactedBy ;
                             rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:Impact ;
                             rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:VulnerableObject ;
                             rdfs:label "has impact on" ;
                             skos:definition "Indicates which vulnerable object is impacted by the specific impact occurrence." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#causesIncidentImpact
beAWARE_ontology:causesIncidentImpact rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                      rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:IncidentType ;
                                      rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:ImpactType ;
                                      rdfs:label "causes incident impact" ;
                                      skos:definition "Indicates which impact type is caused by this type of incident." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#involvedInIncident
beAWARE_ontology:involvedInIncident rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                    owl:inverseOf beAWARE_ontology:involvesParticipant ;
                                    rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:VulnerableObject ;
                                    rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:Incident ;
                                    rdfs:label "involved in incident" ;
                                    skos:definition "Indicates the incident a vulnerable object is involved in." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#isImpactOfIncident
beAWARE_ontology:isImpactOfIncident rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                    rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:Impact ;
                                    rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:Incident ;
                                    rdfs:label "is impact of incident" ;
                                    skos:definition "Links an impact to an incident." .


###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#isImpactedBy
beAWARE_ontology:isImpactedBy rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                              rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:VulnerableObject ;
                              rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:Impact ;
                              rdfs:label "is impacted by" ;
                              skos:definition "Links an affected object to an impact." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#causedByIncident
beAWARE_ontology:causedByIncident rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                  owl:inverseOf beAWARE_ontology:causesIncidentImpact ;
                                  rdfs:domain beAWARE_ontology:ImpactType ;
                                  rdfs:range beAWARE_ontology:IncidentType ;
                                  rdfs:label "caused by incident" ;
                                  skos:definition "Indicates which incident type has caused this impact type." .
###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#VulnerableObject
beAWARE_ontology:VulnerableObject rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                  rdfs:label "Vulnerable Object" ;
                                  skos:definition "Any living being or object that needs to be protected from hazards." .


###  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beAWARE-project/ontology/master/beAWARE_ontology#Wall
beAWARE_ontology:Wall rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf beAWARE_ontology:Structure ;
                      rdfs:label "Wall" ;
                      skos:definition "A vertical structure often made of stone or brick that divides or surrounds something." .
  END OF ONTOLOGY

  COMMON GUIDELINES

  Please try to implement the following guidelines:
  1: Variables e.g., (?something) used in Conclusions must be present in Conditions. So create as many variables as needed in Conditions. 
  2: Each atom must have exactly one variable if it represents class.
  3: Each atom must have exactly two variables if it represents object property.
  4: Each concept must be represented by a class and must be present in Conditions.

  END OF COMMON GUIDELINES

  The rule in natural language is the following: If a vulnerable object and an incident are detected, and this incident has an impact, then the vulnerable object is impacted by that impact.
  Give only the final SWRL rule
